`acl gen` compiles zk2's grant shapes (spec §11.1) from an enrollment and
the contracts into a router's `access_control` block. The enrollment here
is the tcgui pilot's (`examples/zk2/acl/`): two backends, `h-<12hex>/tc`,
and the frontend, a pure consumer bound to `*/tc` that calls every
operation. The contracts are read offline, here from the examples' history
root; the verb opens no session.

The table is the plan as a person reads it: who, bound by what, running
what, with which rules; then every rule with its grant and key expressions.
Own spells each verbatim subtree beside the prefix, because `**` never
crosses one (§1.3). The frontend's selectors are wildcards at the system
position, so each backend's egress grant carries them (`fan-in`), and its
ingress reply too (`fan-in-reply`): egress is checked by inclusion against
the selector itself (§11.2). The frontend reads the backends' presence
(0.8).

```console
$ zenctl acl gen --enrollment ../examples/zk2/acl/tcgui.enrollment.toml --contracts ../examples/zk2/.history --format table
