under default_permission allow, zenoh evaluates no allow rule, so each grant is compiled into denies of its complement, from the contracts given (regenerate on every revision). Deny works by inclusion: a put on a wildcard key is in none and reaches subscribers (R6 discards it), and a wildcard call is in none either, so an operation that allows fan-out executes on every provider for a principal never granted it, only its answers denied (O2 refuses the others). A session matching no subject has no policy and is allowed everything, so authentication must refuse it (§11.2, §11.3)

```

`--explain` answers per direction, with the rules that decided. The
frontend's fan-in GET is its own to send (ingress); toward a backend, the
backend's subject decides, through its fan-in grant (egress).

```console
$ zenctl acl gen --enrollment ../examples/zk2/acl/tcgui.enrollment.toml --contracts ../examples/zk2/.history --format table --explain tcgui-frontend 'zk2/*/tc/tc.netif.v1/state/**' query
tcgui-frontend  query  zk2/*/tc/tc.netif.v1/state/**
  ingress  ALLOWED            consume-in:h-0123456789ab/tcgui-frontend includes it for query on ingress
      ✓ consume-in:h-0123456789ab/tcgui-frontend  allow  zk2/*/tc/tc.netif.v1/state/**  (consume)
  egress   denied by default  no rule of tcgui-frontend's policies includes zk2/*/tc/tc.netif.v1/state/** for query on egress: default_permission deny

```

```console
$ zenctl acl gen --enrollment ../examples/zk2/acl/tcgui.enrollment.toml --contracts ../examples/zk2/.history --format table --explain tc-h-3fa9c2d41b7e 'zk2/*/tc/tc.netif.v1/state/**' query
tc-h-3fa9c2d41b7e  query  zk2/*/tc/tc.netif.v1/state/**
  ingress  denied by default  no rule of tc-h-3fa9c2d41b7e's policies includes zk2/*/tc/tc.netif.v1/state/** for query on ingress: default_permission deny
  egress   ALLOWED            fan-in:h-3fa9c2d41b7e/tc includes it for query on egress
      ✓ fan-in:h-3fa9c2d41b7e/tc  allow  zk2/*/tc/tc.netif.v1/state/**  (fan_in)

```

A backend writing another backend's key: no rule includes it, and the
block's default is deny. Under allow, the complement's deny wins.

```console
$ zenctl acl gen --enrollment ../examples/zk2/acl/tcgui.enrollment.toml --contracts ../examples/zk2/.history --format table --explain tc-h-3fa9c2d41b7e 'zk2/h-20609002f7b6/tc/tc.netif.v1/state/namespaces' put
tc-h-3fa9c2d41b7e  put  zk2/h-20609002f7b6/tc/tc.netif.v1/state/namespaces
  ingress  denied by default  no rule of tc-h-3fa9c2d41b7e's policies includes zk2/h-20609002f7b6/tc/tc.netif.v1/state/namespaces for put on ingress: default_permission deny
  egress   denied by default  no rule of tc-h-3fa9c2d41b7e's policies includes zk2/h-20609002f7b6/tc/tc.netif.v1/state/namespaces for put on egress: default_permission deny

```

```console
$ zenctl acl gen --enrollment ../examples/zk2/acl/tcgui.enrollment.toml --contracts ../examples/zk2/.history --default-permission allow --format table --explain tc-h-3fa9c2d41b7e 'zk2/h-20609002f7b6/tc/tc.netif.v1/state/namespaces' put
under default_permission allow, zenoh evaluates no allow rule, so each grant is compiled into denies of its complement, from the contracts given (regenerate on every revision). Deny works by inclusion: a put on a wildcard key is in none and reaches subscribers (R6 discards it), and a wildcard call is in none either, so an operation that allows fan-out executes on every provider for a principal never granted it, only its answers denied (O2 refuses the others). A session matching no subject has no policy and is allowed everything, so authentication must refuse it (§11.2, §11.3)
tc-h-3fa9c2d41b7e  put  zk2/h-20609002f7b6/tc/tc.netif.v1/state/namespaces
  ingress  DENIED  deny-write:tc-h-3fa9c2d41b7e denies put on ingress; deny wins
      ✗ deny-write:tc-h-3fa9c2d41b7e  deny  zk2/h-20609002f7b6/tc/**  (deny_write)
  egress   DENIED  deny-receive:tc-h-3fa9c2d41b7e denies put on egress; deny wins
      ✗ deny-receive:tc-h-3fa9c2d41b7e  deny  zk2/h-20609002f7b6/tc/tc.netif.v1/state/namespaces  (deny_receive)

```

A principal the plan does not carry is a question that cannot be asked.

```console
$ zenctl acl gen --enrollment ../examples/zk2/acl/tcgui.enrollment.toml --contracts ../examples/zk2/.history --explain nobody 'zk2/**' put
? 2
Error: explain: principal "nobody" is not enrolled; the plan knows ["tc-h-3fa9c2d41b7e", "tc-h-20609002f7b6", "tcgui-frontend"]

```

`--check --against` compares the plan with a router config file, read
through zenoh's own loader: the running block is not observable on the bus
(§11.3). The clean fixture is the plan's own block, wrapped.

```console
$ zenctl acl gen --enrollment ../examples/zk2/acl/tcgui.enrollment.toml --contracts ../examples/zk2/.history --format table --check --against tests/cmd/fixtures/acl/router.json5
tests/cmd/fixtures/acl/router.json5: 18 rule(s) and 3 subject(s) configured; 18 and 3 planned
PASS
compared against the config file tests/cmd/fixtures/acl/router.json5 through zenoh's own loader: access control is enforced per hop and the running configuration is not observable on the bus, so a file that differs from what the router was started with is invisible here (spec §11.3)

```

The drifted one: the fan-in GET's selector dropped from one backend's egress
grant (that GET then gets no reply from it, §11.2), and a subject the
enrollment never enrolled, bound by a zid.

```console
